 neutral company  earn such a terrible reputation  foreign  use cases of PayPal is to pay  third-party sellers that you don't fully  trust if the seller does try to pull a  quick one you would hope that PayPal  would side with the customer and help  facilitate a refund but according to the  vast majority of online anecdotes PayPal  never sides with the customer even when  there are several red flags it and a  bunch of evidence take red hat 27's  experience for example Red Hat tried to  buy one of these wide security cameras  from a third-party seller using PayPal  but as you would guess red hat would  never receive his camera so he would  file a clan with paypala and do some  digging on his own first he tried  calling the seller's phone number and  the person that picked up was some tow  truck driver apparently the tow truck  driver was receiving over 300 calls  every day from people who had fallen for  this scam and he had already reported it  to the police and the Better Business  Bureau you would think that this  information alone was enough for PayPal  to side with red hat but red hat had  even more evidence  he went ahead and called ups and UPS  would confirm that the parcel wasn't  even being shipped to his home address  what this camera did do though was ship  some sort of parcel to the same city and  state this way when you track the  package online where only the city and  state are displayed it looks like the  package was delivered correctly PayPal  would take this as evidence that the  package was indeed delivered and closed  the case against the red hat all PayPal  had to do was call UPS about the  shipment and it would have been clear  who was on the right side but PayPal  couldn't even do that while the vast  majority of complaints surrounding  PayPal are by related it would be unfair  to say that it's only the buyers that  are getting scammed the vast majority of  PayPal customers are buyers and that's  why their stories dominate the internet  but there's plenty of seller horror  stories as well one of these stories  comes from a man named Andy Dollinger  who sells electronics online one day  Andy received an inquiry from a customer  regarding abuse blindership products to  India Andy had no problem shipping  products internationally but he was a  bit concerned about the buyer's account  apparently the account was created  within the last month and only had to  pause the feedbacks nonetheless Andy  decided to give the buyer the benefit of  the doubt and shipped him a 1500 iPhone  12 Pro Max it took some time for the  product to reach the scammer but as soon  as he received it he filed a complaint  that the Box didn't contain the phone  Andy would reach out to DHL who  graciously provided CCTV footage that  proved the weight of the box and he also  contacted his supplier Who provided  evidence that the box with the phone  weighed the same amount and Andy turned  over both pieces of evidence to PayPal  at first it seemed like PayPal actually  sided with Andy but two weeks later Andy  received an email letting him know that  they had decided to side with the buyer  since then and he has called the Indian  police the European consumer Center and  sent over more evidence to PayPal but  none of this has gotten Andy his money  back as you can see PayPal doesn't  necessarily always aside with the buyer  or the seller per se but it does seem  like they usually side with this camera  the reason for this is that the scammers  are keenly aware of exactly how PayPal  conducts their dispute investigations  and they precisely structure their  thoughts to fall through PayPal's gaps  for example the first scammer knew to  send the product to the same city and  state and the second scammer knew to use  a new account without a bad track  history you would think that PayPal  would be aware of these loopholes in  their system and maybe they are but  given the raw number of PayPal horror  stories online it doesn't seem like  they've 